From: dresden196 <50474030+dresden196@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] sepolicy: overlayfs files keep their own labels

NickelOS puts Gentoo's files over /usr and /etc with overlay mounts at
boot (docs/gentoo-at-root.md). The policy said nothing about overlayfs,
so every file seen through them was unlabeled: ChromeOS's own programs
under /usr lost their labels, and with them the domains they run in
(fusermount3 could no longer mount, so AppImages failed). Labeled from
each file's own xattr, as on the filesystems below (Android's policy
does the same for overlayfs): the image's files keep the image's labels,
Gentoo's have their data partition's.

---
diff -ruN a/sepolicy/policy/base/fs_use b/sepolicy/policy/base/fs_use
--- a/sepolicy/policy/base/fs_use
+++ b/sepolicy/policy/base/fs_use
@@ -8,6 +8,7 @@
 fs_use_xattr btrfs u:object_r:labeledfs:s0;
 fs_use_xattr f2fs u:object_r:labeledfs:s0;
 fs_use_xattr squashfs u:object_r:labeledfs:s0;
+fs_use_xattr overlay u:object_r:labeledfs:s0;
 
 # Label inodes from task label.
 fs_use_task pipefs u:object_r:pipefs:s0;
